 17592186045417] [17592186050963 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050963 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050963 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050963 #edb/ref 1031 "Limits of Sequences With Factorials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050963 #edb/ref 1033 1089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050963 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "75afaa59-a005-5d2c-b91f-0d73d0106178" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179514 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5669" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179514 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $n \\geq 2$ is an integer, simplify $\\dfrac {n!} {(n - 2)!}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179514 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6dfde17c-e23e-5137-86cb-f8b756788f13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179514 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice the following:\n\n$$\nn! = n \\cdot (n-1) \\cdot (n-2)!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $(n-2)!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {n!} {(n - 2)!} \n&= \\dfrac {n \\cdot (n - 1) \\cdot (n - 2)!} {(n - 2)!}\\[5pt] \n\n&= \\dfrac {n \\cdot (n - 1) \\cdot \\cancel{(n - 2)!}} {\\cancel{(n - 2)!}}\\[5pt] \n\n&= n(n -1) \\[5pt]\n&= n^2 - n\n\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179515 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5670" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179515 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $k \\geq 1$ is an integer, simplify $\\dfrac {k^2(k - 1)!} {k!}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179515 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "357d19f9-977d-5143-a881-3ecbc068556c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179515 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we notice that \n\n$$\nk! = k \\cdot (k-1)!\n$$\n\nSo, we can simplify the numerator as follows:\n\n$$\nk^2 (k-1)! = k \\cdot \\underbrace{k \\cdot (k-1)!}_{k!} = k \\cdot k!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $k!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {k^2(k - 1)!} {k!} \n&= \\dfrac {k \\cdot k!} {k!}\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac {k \\cdot \\cancel{k!}} {\\cancel{k!}} \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac {k} {1} \\[5pt]\n&= k\n\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179592 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179592 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $(k+1)!$ for any $k \\geq 2?$\n\nI. $(k+1) \\cdot k \\cdot (k-1)$\n\nII. $(k+1) \\cdot k \\cdot (k-1)!$\n\nIII. $(k+1) \\cdot k$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179592 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bcdea8ab-4c41-5d45-b62f-a1f2dc9f83e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179592 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the definition of factorial, we have \n\n$$\n(k+1)! = (k+1) \\cdot k \\cdot (k-1) \\cdot (k-2) \\cdot \\: \\cdots \\: \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first two factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)! & =(k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot \\overset{(k-1)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot (k-1)!\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179659 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179659 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179659 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179659 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179659 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179659 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179659 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179659 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179659 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179659 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179659 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179659 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179659 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186560977 #edb/ref 175