f 1062 #uuid "455da497-9e49-58fc-a08d-ffe137a6ee4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180382 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180478 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180478 #edb/ref 1061 35638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180478 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c241e16e-442e-51d2-908a-ad376a91f3f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180478 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180676 #edb/ref 1064 "Factorials can be applied to variables, too. To manipulate a factorial expression involving a variable, we just need to remember that the variable represents a number, and the previous number can be obtained by subtracting $1$ from the variable.\n\nSo, in the same way that we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5! & =5\\cdot \\overset{4!}{\\overset{⏞}{4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4!\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n! & =n\\cdot \\overset{(n-1)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(n-1)\\cdot (n-2)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =n\\cdot (n-1)!\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes some positive integer.\n\nLikewise, in the same way that we have\n\n$$\n5! = 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3!\n$$\n\nwe also have\n\n$$\nx! = x \\cdot (x-1) \\cdot (x-2)!\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180676 #edb/ref 1065 3885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180676 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d18ec8c1-f094-5d15-ac9e-61e003934f78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180676 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233345 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233345 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124492" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233345 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233345 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $(n-2)!$ for any $n\\ge 4?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233345 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "242ef596-08e0-5569-8454-a2cc5d042d36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233345 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the definition of factorial, we have\n\n$$\n(n-2)!=(n-2)\\cdot (n-3)\\cdot (n-4)\\cdot (n-5)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first two factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n-2)! & =(n-2)\\cdot (n-3)\\cdot \\overset{(n-4)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(n-4)\\cdot (n-5)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(n-2)\\cdot (n-3)\\cdot (n-4)!\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233618 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233618 #edb/ref 1069 "q-19877" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233618 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233618 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $k\\ge 0$ is an integer, $\\frac{{((k+1)!)}^{2}}{k!}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233618 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ce46fc1-2caa-54d2-842e-6b699e777d29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233618 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n(k+1)!=(k+1)k!\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $k!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{((k+1)!)}^{2}}{k!} & =\\frac{{((k+1)k!)}^{2}}{k!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k+1)^{2}\\,\\cdot (k!)^{2}}{k!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k+1)^{2}\\,\\cdot k!\\cdot \\cancel{k!}}{\\cancel{k!}} \\\\  & =(k+1)^{2}k!\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233619 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233619 #edb/ref 1069 "q-19882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233619 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233619 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $n\\ge 9$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n-8)!\\,(n+9)!}{(n-9)!\\,(n+7)!}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233619 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f3ef0665-6f2a-5dd9-af17-7da892c3b332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233619 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n+9)! & =(n+9)(n+8)(n+7)! \\\\ (n-8)! & =(n-8)(n-9)!\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $(n+7)!$ and $(n-9)!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n-8)!\\,(n+9)!}{(n-9)!\\,(n+7)!} & =\\frac{[(n-8)\\cdot (n-9)!]\\cdot [(n+9)\\cdot (n+8)\\cdot (n+7)!]}{(n-9)!\\cdot (n+7)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n-8)\\cdot \\cancel{(n-9)!}\\cdot (n+9)\\cdot (n+8)\\cdot \\cancel{(n+7)!}}{\\cancel{(n-9)!}\\cdot \\cancel{(n+7)!}} \\\\  & =(n-8)\\cdot (n+8)\\cdot (n+9) \\\\  & =(n-8)(n+8)(n+9)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234328 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234328 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234328 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234328 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $n\\ge 1$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n-1)!}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234328 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "81d120ad-3169-5d64-9a97-a76dfeab46ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234328 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n(n+1)!=(n+1)\\cdot n\\cdot (n-1)!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $(n-1)!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n-1)!} & =\\frac{(n+1)\\cdot n\\cdot (n-1)!}{(n-1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n+1)\\cdot n\\cdot \\cancel{(n-1)!}}{\\cancel{(n-1)!}} \\\\  & =n(n+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258245 #edb/ref 1080 "(n-2)\\cdot (n-3)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258245 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258245 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a7d70344-02ef-576e-88c3-b1257e9fa69d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258315 #edb/ref 1080 "(n-2)\\cdot (n-4)!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258315 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258315 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "afe91a2d-c0e2-5aba-9506-7d7fa44f831a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258490 #edb/ref 1080 "(n-2)\\cdot (n-3)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258490 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417]