86045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560969 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560969 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186560634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560969 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fc7e4408-fbc9-5f18-bf91-1f672892b184" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560977 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560977 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122060" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560977 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560977 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $n\\ge 2$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n-2)!(n+1)!}{(n!)^{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560977 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14a5adf6-2b28-57e0-9971-452cd7b77a19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560977 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n! & =n\\cdot (n-1)\\cdot (n-2)! \\\\ (n+1)! & =(n+1)\\cdot n!\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $(n-2)!$ and $n!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n-2)!(n+1)!}{(n!)^{2}} & =\\frac{(n-2)!(n+1)!}{n!\\cdot n!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n-2)!\\cdot (n+1)\\cdot n!}{n!\\cdot n\\cdot (n-1)\\cdot (n-2)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(n-2)!}\\cdot (n+1)\\cdot \\cancel{n!}}{\\cancel{n!}\\cdot n\\cdot (n-1)\\cdot \\cancel{(n-2)!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{n+1}{n(n-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561023 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561023 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79305" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561023 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561023 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $m\\ge 1$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(m+2)!}{(m+2)\\text{ }(m-1)!}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561023 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8948ecd0-03b4-5bf8-9bc2-1467d2b61321" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561023 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n(m+2)!=(m+2)(m+1)(m)(m-1)!\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $(m+2)$ and $(m-1)!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(m+2)!}{(m+2)\\text{ }(m-1)!} & =\\frac{(m+2)\\cdot (m+1)\\cdot m\\cdot (m-1)!}{(m+2)\\cdot (m-1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(m+2)}\\cdot (m+1)\\cdot m\\cdot \\cancel{(m-1)!}}{\\cancel{(m+2)}\\cdot \\cancel{(m-1)!}} \\\\  & =(m+1)\\cdot m \\\\  & ={m}^{2}+m\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561038 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561038 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124490" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561038 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561038 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $(k-1)!$ for any $k\\ge 4?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561038 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a8478761-1f4e-5c9d-a634-3425df7889e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561038 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the definition of factorial, we have \n\n$$\n(k-1)!=(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot (k-3)\\cdot (k-4)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first two factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k-1)! & =(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot \\overset{(k-3)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(k-3)\\cdot (k-4)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot (k-3)!\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561046 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561046 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122049" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561046 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561046 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $n\\ge 0$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n+3)!}{n!}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561046 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c9507815-c165-5ef4-b7fc-1303dcd3fdc4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561046 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n(n+3)!=(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)\\cdot (n+1)\\cdot n!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $n!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n+3)!}{n!} & =\\frac{(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)\\cdot (n+1)\\cdot n!}{n!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)\\cdot (n+1)\\cdot \\cancel{n!}}{\\cancel{n!}} \\\\  & =(n+1)(n+2)(n+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
